  <section id="chto-eto">
    <h2>How nerves are injured</h2>
    <p>A nerve is like a cable made of many fibres in a common sheath. An injury can:</p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>bruise or stretch it</strong> — the sheath is intact, the fibres temporarily stop conducting. It often recovers on its own;</li>
      <li><strong>cut it partly or completely</strong> — with cuts from glass, knives, metal, open fractures. It will not heal by itself: repair is needed;</li>
      <li><strong>compress it</strong> with scar tissue, fracture callus or a haematoma.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>Arm nerves are injured most often — median, ulnar, radial — especially with cuts at the wrist and forearm.</p>
  </section>

  <section id="simptomy">
    <h2>Signs</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>after an injury the fingers or an area of skin went numb;</li>
      <li>fingers, the hand or the foot will not bend or straighten;</li>
      <li>touching the injury site sends an electric jolt;</li>
      <li>over time the muscles waste.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>If any of this appears after a cut, the nerve is most likely damaged, even if the wound is small.</p>
  </section>

  <section id="diagnostika">
    <h2>Diagnosis</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Examination</strong> — the area of numbness and which movements are lost show which nerve is injured.</li>
      <li><strong>Nerve ultrasound</strong> — shows whether the nerve is intact, where it is torn and how far the ends have separated.</li>
      <li><strong>Nerve conduction study</strong> — assesses conduction. Signs of injury do not appear immediately, so the test is sometimes repeated after a few weeks.</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section id="lechenie">
    <h2>Treatment</h2>
    <h3>Nerve repair</h3>
    <p>When a nerve is cut, its ends are trimmed under the microscope — the damaged edges are removed — and sewn together with the finest sutures through the sheath. The sooner this is done, the better the result: over time the nerve ends retract and scar, and the muscles left without a nerve weaken.</p>
    <figure class="case-fig">
      <figcaption>A case from my practice: a man cut his hand on metal, and the emergency department stitched the skin. A week and a half later, at surgery, the nerve turned out to be almost completely cut, with the ends 3–4 cm apart. I trimmed the damaged edges and repaired the nerve. <a href={`${tg}/138`} target="_blank" rel="noopener">The story and video are on my Telegram channel</a> (in Russian).</figcaption>
    </figure>
    <h3>Nerve grafting</h3>
    <p>If the ends cannot be brought together without tension, the gap is bridged with a graft from the patient's own thin sensory nerve.</p>
    <h3>Neurolysis</h3>
    <p>If the nerve is intact but compressed by scar or fracture callus, it is freed.</p>
    <h3>Observation</h3>
    <p>With a bruised or stretched nerve without rupture, patients are usually observed with repeat nerve conduction studies, and surgery is considered if recovery does not progress.</p>
  </section>

  <section id="vosstanovlenie">
    <h2>Recovery</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>After repair the arm or leg is immobilised for a while so the nerve is not stretched and heals properly.</li>
      <li>Nerves grow slowly — on average about a millimetre a day. Recovery takes months.</li>
      <li>Rehabilitation matters: exercise therapy supervised by a physiotherapist, sensory retraining and braces if needed.</li>
      <li>A follow-up nerve conduction study shows how recovery is going.</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

